 Depends which “read-only JMX” you mean.

  For the JDK jmxremote.access readonly role, nodetool only works for commands that use JMX attribute reads/metadata. Common ones that should work in this checkout:

  version, info, netstats, tpstats, tablestats, tablehistograms, datapaths, proxyhistograms, compactionhistory, compactionstats, clientstats without --clear-history, gcstats, gossipinfo, failuredetector, cidrfilteringstats,
  getauthcacheconfig, getautorepairconfig, getauditlog, getbatchlogreplaythrottle, getcolumnindexsize, getcompactionthreshold, getcompactionthroughput, getconcurrentcompactors, getconcurrentviewbuilders, getdefaultrf,
  getfullquerylog, getinterdcstreamthroughput, getlogginglevels, getmaxhintwindow, getseeds, getsnapshotthrottle, getstreamthroughput, gettimeout, gettraceprobability, getguardrailsconfig, statusbackup, statusbinary,
  statusgossip, statushandoff.

  Commands like status, ring, and describecluster are “read-like” but call JMX operations such as effectiveOwnershipWithPort, so a plain JDK readonly user can fail there.

  For Cassandra integrated JMX authorization, the docs recommend:

  GRANT SELECT ON ALL MBEANS TO jmx;
  GRANT DESCRIBE ON ALL MBEANS TO jmx;
  GRANT EXECUTE ON MBEAN 'org.apache.cassandra.db:type=EndpointSnitchInfo' TO jmx;
  GRANT EXECUTE ON MBEAN 'org.apache.cassandra.db:type=StorageService' TO jmx;

  That is specifically documented to make read-only tooling, including nodetool status, work. See doc/modules/cassandra/pages/managing/operating/security.adoc:526. The permission mapping is in src/java/org/apache/cassandra/auth/
  jmx/AuthorizationProxy.java:418: attributes need SELECT, setters need MODIFY, operations need EXECUTE.




 how allow readonly jmx user1 nodetool status operation?


• Use Cassandra integrated JMX authorization, then grant user1 the read permissions plus the two EXECUTE grants that nodetool status needs.

  In cqlsh as a superuser:

  CREATE ROLE IF NOT EXISTS jmx_readonly WITH LOGIN = false;

  GRANT SELECT ON ALL MBEANS TO jmx_readonly;
  GRANT DESCRIBE ON ALL MBEANS TO jmx_readonly;

  GRANT EXECUTE ON MBEAN 'org.apache.cassandra.db:type=EndpointSnitchInfo' TO jmx_readonly;
  GRANT EXECUTE ON MBEAN 'org.apache.cassandra.db:type=StorageService' TO jmx_readonly;

  GRANT jmx_readonly TO user1;

  Then run:

  bin/nodetool -u user1 -pw '<password>' status

  If user1 already has an active JMX permissions cache entry, either wait for cache expiry or invalidate it as an admin:

  bin/nodetool invalidatejmxpermissionscache user1

  Make sure Cassandra is using integrated JMX authz, not only the JDK jmxremote.access file. In cassandra-env.sh, this is the relevant setting:

  JVM_OPTS="$JVM_OPTS -Dcassandra.jmx.authorizer=org.apache.cassandra.auth.jmx.AuthorizationProxy"

  Caveat: EXECUTE ON MBEAN 'org.apache.cassandra.db:type=StorageService' is broader than just status; Cassandra authorizes JMX operation execution at the MBean level, not per operation.





Under a readonly JMX role (jmxremote.access), MBeanServerAccessController permits getAttribute(s), queryNames/queryMBeans, getMBeanInfo, isInstanceOf, addNotificationListener — and blocks invoke, setAttribute(s), createMBean. Cassandra's own JMX authorizer maps the same split (src/java/org/apache/cassandra/auth/jmx/AuthorizationProxy.java:422): getAttribute→SELECT, setAttribute→MODIFY, invoke→EXECUTE, queryNames/getMBeanInfo→DESCRIBE.

NodeProbe reaches the server through JMX.newMBeanProxy, and MBeanServerInvocationHandler decides attribute-vs-invoke purely from the Java interface signature: a no-arg getX()/isX() returning non-void is an attribute read; everything else — including any getter that takes an argument — is invoke. That's the whole discriminator.

NodeProbe.connect() itself is safe: proxy creation is lazy, and the only server calls are isInstanceOf via newPlatformMXBeanProxy.

